// Reference / CLI / mcp.
//
// Written rather than generated: the binary's own surface. The MCP tool list
// itself is generated and lives elsewhere, so this page covers the server and
// how to attach a client to it, and does not restate the tools.
//
// No tool count here on purpose: verify-reference-ia rejects a hand-written
// one, because a number typed into prose is a number that goes stale.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CliBlock from '../../../components/docs/CliBlock.astro';

const claudeConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "strata": {
      "command": "strata",
      "args": ["/path/to/my-db", "mcp", "serve"]
    }
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  editPath="src/pages/docs/reference/mcp.astro"
  title="mcp"
  description="Serve Strata over the Model Context Protocol, so an agent can use a database as tools rather than by shelling out."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>mcp</h1>
  <p>
    <code>strata mcp serve</code> exposes a database over the Model Context Protocol, so an MCP client
    can read and write it as tools instead of composing shell commands.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db mcp serve</CliBlock>
  <p>
    It speaks newline-delimited JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout, and puts its logs on stderr. There is
    no port and no URL: the client starts the process and talks to it over the pipe, which is why
    the command looks like it does nothing when you run it in a terminal. That is correct. It is
    waiting for a client.
  </p>

  <h2>Naming the database</h2>
  <p>
    The database comes from the usual <a href="/docs/reference/global-options">global options</a>,
    before the subcommand. Both of these serve the same database:
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db mcp serve strata --db ./my-db mcp serve</CliBlock>
  <p>
    <code>--branch</code> and <code>--space</code> work here too, so a server can be pinned to one branch
    for the whole session.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db --branch review mcp serve</CliBlock>

  <h2>Attaching a client</h2>
  <p>
    Configuration lives with the client, not with Strata. A stdio MCP server is normally declared as
    a command and its arguments:
  </p>
  <CliBlock variant="output">{claudeConfig}</CliBlock>
  <p>
    Use an absolute path for the database. The client decides the working directory it launches the
    process in, and a relative path will be resolved against a directory you did not choose.
  </p>

  <h2>What it exposes</h2>
  <p>
    Every command in the catalog, as its own tool. Each carries a tool name and a description
    written for a model deciding whether to call it, so <code>admin.config</code> arrives as
    <code>strata_admin_config</code> with a sentence about when to reach for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The list is generated from the release rather than written here, so it matches the binary you
    have installed. Ask the binary with <code>strata agents commands</code>, or read the <a
      href="/docs/reference">command reference</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing the database with your shell</h2>
  <p>
    An MCP server holds the database open for as long as the client keeps it running, which raises
    the obvious question of what happens when you also want a terminal on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing special: the default <code>--ipc host</code> means the first process to open a durable database
    hosts a socket, and the next one is brokered to it rather than refused. A shell opened against the
    same path while the server is running just works.
  </p>
  <p>
    What <a href="/docs/reference/start-and-stop">start</a> adds is deciding <em>which</em> process is
    the owner, instead of it being whichever opened the database first. That matters here, because the
    owner's durability mode is the one that applies to everyone brokered to it.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
